    {
      "id": 33,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Direct-SQL changes (reserved)",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 24
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**This panel is intentionally empty.**\n\nEvery service connects to Postgres as `dcms`, a cluster superuser. ADR 0007 is blunt that the append-only trigger and the `REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE` on the audit tables stop *accidents*, not *attackers*: anyone holding the application's credentials can turn them off. The audit log records every change the application makes, and by construction it cannot see a change made with `psql`.\n\nClosing that gap needs logical-replication CDC: a row change with no matching audit record is, by definition, a write that bypassed the application. The metric name is reserved — `dcms.cdc.unaudited_change` — and the design is in `docs/adr/0008-observability.md`. It is deliberately not built yet; `wal_level` is still `replica` and turning it on costs WAL volume on a disk-constrained host for no benefit until something reads the slot.\n\nUntil then this is a **known gap**, recorded here rather than left implied."
      }
    }
